<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Silius Italicus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 26–c. 101</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Silius Italicus was a Roman senator and poet, consul in 68 CE, who wrote the Punica, an epic in seventeen books on the Second Punic War and the longest surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poem. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-younger">Pliny the Younger</a> described his wealthy retirement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> and his death by starvation when he fell incurably ill. The Punica preserves many traditions about the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silius-italicus.jpg" alt="Silius Italicus" width="320" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Wright of Derby · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Wright_of_Derby_-_Virgil%27s_Tomb_by_Moonlight,_with_Silius_Italicus_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
